Well, your information is very rare...

"...network is down..."
Of course, you cannot login as a user whose passwd is serverd by nis. This
means you have to log in as somebody still remaining in your local
/etc/passwd. For root, this should be the case.

When  "...network is down..." has the meaning, that only your
MASTER-nis-server is down, there may be the chance to have a SLAVE-nis
server. If so, log in the client as root an set declare the
slave-nis-server using the gui-tool in the administration-box

If you have no slave-server: Check /etc wether there is an old
/etc/passwd-file from ancient times (with declared users in; it should have
no "+" in the last line) and replace this for the current /etc/passwd
(Don't forget to restore that one to antother file before...). So then,
your machine is standing-allone again and you can work on it , till your
network is up again. If you haven't an old /etc/passwd, you may edit the
current /etc/passwd by hand for you as a user for spanning the serverless
time.

Some problem may  occur, if your /home/user - files are shared over network
by using nfs (via an automounter ?). In this case , you have no chance to
get your  userfiles as long as the network is down.
If you use nfs, and use it stationary ( no automounter ) , you should have
the "bg" option in every entry in the /etc/fstab concerning a partitition
served via the network. Why? "bg" means background. If this option isn't
set, your machine will hang at boot time, when the nfs-partitions are tried
to be mounted. With this option set, the mounting of this partitions will
be moved into the background and will not disturb the ongoing of the
boot-process.
In this case the login-process will take some time (several minutes),
because the machine misses your "home"-dir. But loggin in should be
possible, dropping you to some shell. Of course, working properly isn't
possible. You could supply a meanwhile-/home/user-dir for the time nfs
doesn't work.

 Note, that you may have  to start repairing from the single-user-mode
("Blue screen"  with promt:>> ).
In this level no partition is mounted!  "show dev" will print a list of
possible partitions to be mounted to the screen. Try to mount your
root-partition and the edit the files using the vi-editor.

Hope I could give you some ideas--good luck!
Olli



